course: dictionary
lesson: 2830
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can find the right one by reading the words around it, which is its context. «The Mongol fleets failed, partly because of storms the Japanese called kamikaze, divine wind.» Here divine is about God or a god, not about something pretty or tasty.
explain: Divine can also mean wonderful. Imagine a grown-up says, «This chocolate pudding is simply divine!» Nobody thinks the pudding came from God. They mean it tastes amazing. The clue is the pudding and the word simply.
explain: Divine can be a verb too. To divine something is to guess or discover it by a feeling. Water divining is when someone uses a stick to look for water underground, but this has not been shown to work in fair tests. Use the clues around the word to choose the meaning that fits.
fact: Divine means of God or a god, or coming from God, as in «divine love» or «divine help».
fact: Divine can mean wonderful or delicious, as in «This cake is divine!»
fact: To divine is to guess or discover something by a feeling.
fact: The French carol «Il est né, le divin enfant» means "He is born, the divine child", and it is hundreds of years old.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The Japanese called the storms kamikaze, divine wind.» In this sentence, divine means | *Coming from a god | Pretty and gentle | Very hot | Made of wood
exercise: «This chocolate pudding is simply divine!» What does divine mean here? | Cold and wet | *Wonderful and delicious | Sad and lonely | Made of stone
exercise: To divine something means to | To eat it slowly | *Guess or discover it by a feeling | Hide it from other people | Paint it bright red
test: In the carol «Il est né, le divin enfant», divin means | Noisy | Lost | Very small | *Holy, of God
test: Which sentence uses divine to mean wonderful? | The divine child was born in a stable | Divine help came to the village | Water divining needs a stick | *The cake was simply divine!
exam: Which is the best way to work out the meaning of a new word like divine? | *Read the words around it and try the meaning in the sentence | Skip it and never come back | Look only at the word's first letter | Guess a random meaning and stop
